WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
A Docker image is a security-relevant build artifact, not just a deployment package.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:16.680
It is the packaged result of source code, dependency restore, build output, base image selection, file copying, metadata, and configuration decisions.

3
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:22.700
By the time an image reaches production, many trust decisions have already been made.

4
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:36.820
An ASP.NET Core image can contain application assemblies, runtime dependencies, native libraries, certificates, labels, default environment assumptions, diagnostic files, generated assets, and accidentally included local files.

5
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.920
Some of those contents are required.

6
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.300
Some are convenient.

7
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.360
Some are leftovers that no one intended to ship.

8
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:51.320
Image security affects supply chain risk, runtime attack surface, patching, reproducibility, and incident response.

9
00:00:51.420 --> 00:01:02.140
If a team cannot explain what is in an image, where it came from, how it was built, and who can publish it, the team will struggle to patch it, investigate it, or prove what ran in production.

10
00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.340
Containers do not make insecure application logic safe.

11
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:17.640
An ASP.NET Core application with weak authorization, unsafe secret handling, poor validation, or excessive data exposure remains weak inside a container.

12
00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:23.340
Image hygiene supports secure deployment, but it does not replace secure application design.

13
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:27.620
A secure image design starts with basic questions.

14
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.320
What base image is used.

15
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.860
What files are copied.

16
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.360
Which dependencies are present.

17
00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.700
Which user runs the process.

18
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.440
Which ports and writable paths are required.

19
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.060
Who can rebuild the image.

20
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.160
Who can sign or publish it.

21
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.460
What metadata proves the source and version.

22
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:51.000
These questions turn a black-box artifact into a reviewable engineering output.
